The starter UI keeps to its bars and chat buttons in a compact main window

0048's narrow layout still puts the whole dock on a phone, map included, at the foot of the screen, where it is a
desktop interface squeezed into a third of it and the app already shows the map itself.

- In a main window whose width or height size class is compact (BaseUI.compactWindow()), it shows no dock and no map:
  only its bars, along the top, and a button 48 logical pixels tall for each chat tab (All, Tells, Channels) along the
  bottom, both locked Adjustable containers attached to their borders. This replaces 0048's narrow and short layouts;
  any other main window is laid out as before 0048.
- A button opens its tab over the game's text and closes it again, shows the tab's unread count, and tells a screen
  reader both ("Tells chat, 2 new", "Tells chat, open"). The bars are named for a screen reader in both layouts, and
  the strips are read as no button though they take clicks (so a right-click opens no menu that would unlock them).
- The bars run two to a row with the window upright and in one row on its side; the strips are sized in logical
  pixels by getMainWindowInfo()'s pixelScale.
- It follows the window across the size boundary on resizes and sysMainWindowClassChangeEvent, keeping chat capture,
  unread counts and the welcome's refit (0044). Version 1.9.8, the mpackage rebuilt.

Tested by UI_spec.lua (the size classes, no dock or map, the bars along the top, two to a row or one row, the buttons
opening and closing their tabs over the console, unread counts and screen reader names, the pixel scale, buttons no
less than 48 logical pixels tall at any scale, the dock back on the right, and a moved dock left alone).

Carried by Mudlet/android's scripts/apply-mudlet-patches.sh until Mudlet/Mudlet has it.
Upstream: not opened yet

